Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the numerator is $x^6$, let's divide by $x^6$.

In the denominator, let's divide by $\sqrt{x^{12}}$, since for any value, $x^6=\sqrt{x^{12}}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{9x^6}{\sqrt{9x^{12}+4x^6}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{9x^6}{x^6}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{9x^{12}+4x^6}}{\sqrt{x^{12}}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x^6=\sqrt{x^{12}}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{9\cancel{x^6}}{\cancel{x^6}}}{\sqrt{\dfrac{9\cancel{x^{12}}}{\cancel{x^{12}}}+\dfrac{4\cancel {x^6}}{\cancel {x^6}\cdot x^6}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{9}{\sqrt{9+\dfrac{4}{x^6}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{9}{\sqrt{9+0}}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{9}{\sqrt{9}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{9}{3}
\\\\
&=3
\end{align}$
